WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:02.470 align:start position:0%
 
thank you very much and good the good  evening members questioners officers of  the council and members of the public  watching live i welcome you to this  question and answer session  as of course you all know our regular  full council meeting was cancelled for  covert reasons nevertheless we have our  question and answer session tonight  especially in order to assure democratic  process  i would ask participants when you are  not speaking to please mute your  microphone this minimizes background  noise and will help everyone in  listening to the proceedings the number  of people who have submitted a question  have asked their question be read out by  a council officer on their behalf  all those who submitted a question  however we'll receive a written response  anyway  so opening straight on to question  number one regarding government funding  in margate from mrs johnston  please can i ask mr clapson from  democratic services to read out the  question on mrs  mrs johnson's behalf  thank you chair so the question is there  have been numerous public reports of  government funding for margate  throughout 2020 to date  could the leader please confirm how much  of that funding has gone to consultants  and how much has been received and given  to projects  meetings of all meet minutes of all  meetings where tdc is the accountable  body would be appreciated  thank you  thank you mr cleveson i call upon  councillor pugh to respond  thank you chair  the margate town deal is the only  funding program specifically  specifically for margate the council is  the accountable body for this program  and there is a significant amount of  information available on the margate  town deal web pages  www.margatetowndeal.co.uk  this includes minutes of meetings the  decision-making process how the town  investment plan was developed amongst  other information  the council was originally allocated 162  thousand and 19 pounds  capacity funding this was to set up the  town deal board and facilitate the  development of a town investment plan  including engaging sp uh engaging  specialist consultancy services  the council then bid for further  capacity funding and received seventy  thousand pounds  the capacity funding is aimed at the  development of the strategic plan and to  support the development of business  cases  so far 143  291 pounds of funding has been used to  support this work with a further 48 000  pounds being commissioned  this is to ensure that the council town  deal board and project sponsors and  delivery partners have the specialist  advice and expertise required to deliver  information needed from the ministry of  housing communities and local government  the capacity funding was not to deliver  projects  the allocation of up to 22.2 million is  for the final development and delivery  of projects  this capital funding will be allocated  once the business cases have been  completed and summary documents sent to  central government for sign off  margate was allocated 750 000  pounds as a town do area for a town  centre capital project the council is  about to let a contract for works on  51-57 high street market  the works include the replacement of the  roof ensuring the building is watertight  protecting it from further deterioration  and the strip out of the internal  materials and fittings that will enable  access to the asbestos containing  materials that require removal  the council also received funding for  reopening high streets safely to the  value of 126  456 pounds  this was spent across six areas in fact  not just margate on specific activities  thank you very much indeed councillor  pugh  question number two regarding the  garland pavilion from miss edwards  please can i call upon mr clapson again  to read out the question on miss  edward's behalf  mr clapson  thank you chad so this one is garland  residents association would like to have  a meeting to discuss the garland  pavilion which is on the asset list  they have contacted the council with no  replies or answers about making this  into a community center to bring all  garland residents together and have a  community space which we can use and be  proud of thank you  thank you mr clapton i call again on  council queue to respond council pew  thank you chair  the garnish pavilion on capstone road is  a small sports pavilion comprising of  eight changing cubicles toilets showers  and a referee's office  there would not be sufficient space for  community centre  this property also requires a further  work of research into its occupancy  before it would be ready and suitable  for viewings or transfer  however once this work has taken place  we can get back in touch with ward  councillors and community groups  thank you again  councillor pugh uh  moving then on to question number three  regarding an environmental forum from  miss pardo  please can i again ask mr clapton to  read that question on miss pardo's  behalf mr clapton thank you  so following the last ipcc report it has  become even clearer that climate change  is a threat we must tackle now  there are many environmental groups  currently working in planet i.e fanet xr  vanet friends of the earth rise up clean  up among others  would it be possible to create an  environmental forum in which all groups  of the council can work together on  future environmental changes ideas to  tackle future threats educational  resources etc thank you  thank you mr clapson i call upon council  cop to respond councillor cobb  thank you chair and thank you uh for  this question this administration takes  climate change seriously and we  recognize that there are groups in the  community working on environmental  issues that have a wealth of knowledge  and skills  we want to work more closely with them  in the future  an environmental forum is an excellent  idea and is something that tdc aspires  to however at present we do not have the  resources to start run and manage a full  forum instead we hope to invite a couple  of members from each environmental group  to an initial meeting in order to start  a discussion and share knowledge and  ideas we hope to do this before  christmas  in the meantime we have a number of  officers whose role is to work with the  community a biodiversity and  horticulture officer litter education  officer  coastal birds officer and athenic coast  projects officer all of these are on  hand to advise community groups in  environmental action we have also  employed a home energy officer who can  assist with reducing home energy costs  and emissions for educational resources  please see our website pages where you  can find information on what climate  change is and what you can do to help  we are currently drawing up strategies  with the associated action plans to  tackle climate change and although we  are in an emergency these take time and  if they are to be meaningful and  successful  we are particularly concentrating on our  greenhouse gas emissions our electricity  gas petrol and diesel use  use and would ask groups to also make  plans to reduce these emissions as only  then will we be able to achieve net zero  and keep temperature rise below 2  degrees celsius  we are also taking practical action as  quickly as possible such as changing to  renewable electricity contracts when  they come up for renewal and changing  the roots of our vehicles to reduce  diesel use there are many other actions  described on our web pages so i would  encourage everyone to look them up by  typing climate change into the tdc  website search bar and clicking on the  first option energy and climate change  we hope that the strategies and action  plans have been produced next summer  we will have the resources to work even  more closely with community groups on  climate change as only by working  together will we be able to reach net  zero and combat the ecological crisis  thank you very much indeed councillor  cub  i now have uh question number four  regarding southern water and future  housing developments from mrs mccourt uh  mrs mccourt will be reading her question  and i'm pleased to welcome her to the  meeting  please turn on your microphone and read  out the wording of the question that you  have submitted to the council  mrs mccourt  thank you  southern water has a history of showing  an utter disregard for the protection of  our marine environments for which tdc  has the responsibility to protect  as the local plan develops  regular discharges of untreated  southern water can't cope with managing  our wastewater safely now and will fare  no better once the proposed 17 000 homes  are built  what exactly does tdc propose to do to  ensure  no further damage is caused as a result  of sewage discharges by southern water  into our marine protected areas  and uphold their responsibility in  protecting our marine life as written in  our local plan  thank you very much indeed mrs mccourt a  concern for all of us  i call upon the leader to respond that's  flashback  thank you chair and thank you mrs mccall  for your question um water companies  including southern water are required to  comply with statutes such as the water  framework directive and bathing water  directive  the environment agency is the regulated  agency for the purposes of these  regulations this is not a function for  tdc as a local authority  the local plan refers to the high level  strategy for dealing with foul and  surface water from new developments  actions regarding this  for example the use of sustainable  drainage solutions for surface water  will be dealt with  through the planning process for  developments  i can also assure you that i am in  regular contact with the chief executive  of southern water and this council takes  this matter very seriously  and we are constantly  referring to  the southern water as to  new um studies that they are doing so i  can assure residents that we are  doing everything um that we can to keep  a close eye on the situation thank you  thank you very much indeed ashby  moving on again question number five  regarding the local plan from mr scott  please can i ask again mr clapton to  read out the question on behalf of mr  scott mr clapson  thank you jeff  what does councillor ashley say to those  in rural areas that will be negatively  affected by the local plan and to those  who include poverty after all after all  the local plan will be destroying land  that produces over 10 million loaves of  bread  thank you mr clapson back to your leader  council ashby  thank you chair and thank you for the  question  the local plan is prepared in accordance  with the government guide governance  guide guidance  that guidance in the national planning  policy framework does not provide  blanket protection for agricultural land  it only states that where significant  development of agricultural land is  necessary areas of poor quality land  should be preferred to those of higher  quality  this has to be balanced against the  important objective of meeting future  housing needs and other government  guidance  it would be more appropriate to direct  this question to the national government  as a national food strategy covering  food security and food poverty  could be given additional weight in the  planning system if changes were made to  the national planning policy framework  to provide greater protection for  agricultural land  for myself personally as leader of the  council  i am writing to the secretary of state  to ask whether or not they will consider  having another close look at the  agricultural policy so i can assure you  that from authentic perspective we are  trying to deal with this on a national  strategy level this is a national  strategy issue  so i hope that you can also  write to the secretary of state yourself  with your concerns thank you  thank you very much councillor ashby  question number six regarding the  council's relationship with gladman  development limited uh from mr woodward  again uh can i please ask mr clapson to  read out the question on mr woodward's  behalf  mr clapson thank you chair  could the council please explain its  perpetual invasiveness and lack of  transparency towards questions asked  about all aspects of its relationship  with gladman developments limited  especially when applications from them  are both highly emotive and contentious  for the residents of planet thank you  thank you again mr clapton i call again  on the leader councillor ashby to  respond councillor ashby  thank you chair and thank you for the  question  we have received a number of requests  under the freedom of information about  this site we have received some  questions about meetings between gladman  and the council and other questions that  have been about other issues to do with  this application  the council has answered these requests  as openly as possible bearing in mind  that pre-application discussions between  the applicant and the council are  confidential  however the outcome of all discussions  and negotiations between officers of the  council and gladman during the course of  the planning application are outlined in  the three separate planning committee  reports all of which are available  online for viewing  thank you very much indeed leader  council ashby  well  councillors officers members of the  public that concludes this question and  answer session thank you to everyone in  attendance and those who have watched  from home  good night

